/-! # Unramified function-field extensions of a normal integral scheme

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BQK (lemma-unramified-in-L) and 0BQM (proposition-normal)
(blueprint: lem-dl-a1-normal-unramified-galois-presentation).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.PreGaloisCategory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale

/-- Normalization is unramified exactly when finite etale. The union of these finite
extensions is Galois, and restriction to it is the geometric-generic fundamental-group
quotient, including its topology and the finite-Galois factorization criterion.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BQK and 0BQM
(blueprint: lem-dl-a1-normal-unramified-galois-presentation).

`G` is a small fundamental-group model of the finite-etale fibre functor. The comparison
`exists_fiber_flc_equivalence` identifies it with the automorphisms of the recorded FLC
fibre functor and their evaluation topology. The equality specifying `q` uses the actual
geometric-generic action, rather than an arbitrary surjection. The membership equivalence
for `M` is the union itself, not just the field generated by that union. The equations on
`a : M` and `a : L` specify restriction as a field map; thus the last existential is
precisely continuous factorization of restriction. No Noetherian assumption is made.

Proof, following the node's eight steps:
1. On each nonempty affine Spec A, the integral closure B of A in L is a domain and
localizes to L: clear a common denominator in a monic equation for each element of L.
The affine normalizations glue, giving an integral, connected source dominating X.
2. The dominant unramified normal-target lemma makes an unramified normalization etale.
Conversely etale implies unramified. An integral locally finite type map is finite,
by `pqf-lem-integral-lft-finite`, proving the three equivalent conditions.
3. `lem-dl-a1-field-fundamental-galois` and `thm-galois-fundamental` identify q with
pullback of finite covers. `lem-sbc-normal-integral-generic-hom-full` makes this fully
faithful. If the compact image were proper, the closed-subgroup/open-overgroup lemma
would give a nontrivial finite coset set with an image-fixed point. Fullness extends
that point to a G-fixed point, contradicting transitivity. Thus q is surjective.
4. Write N = ker q. A continuous surjection from a compact group to a Hausdorff group
is a quotient map. Every finite continuous action trivial on N therefore descends to G.
5. For finite L, apply the fundamental equivalence to Hom(L, Ω). Finite etaleness makes
N act trivially. Conversely descend the action and obtain a finite etale cover with
generic fibre Spec L. `lem-dl-a1-finite-etale-normalization-components` identifies this
cover with the normalization in the specified L.
6. Put E = Ω^N. The transitivity in `lem-exc-etale-field-galois-classification` and
normality of N show that N acts trivially on Hom(L, Ω) exactly when L lies in E.
Every element of E lies in its finite simple subextension, so the union is exactly E.
7. `lem-weil-pbc-separable-closure-exists-galois` and
`lem-dl-a1-galois-fixed-normal-quotient` give E/F Galois and the restriction quotient.
The resulting isomorphism G -> Gal(E/F) is continuous by the quotient topology of q.
The Krull finite-limit description makes its target Hausdorff; compactness of G then
makes its inverse continuous as well.
8. For finite Galois L, restriction factors continuously through q exactly when N
fixes L. Necessity is immediate; sufficiency descends restriction along the quotient
map. This is L contained in E, hence exactly unramifiedness by steps 5 and 6. -/
theorem normal_unramified_galois_presentation
    {X : Scheme.{u}} [IsIntegral X]
    (hX : ∀ x : X, IsIntegrallyClosed (X.presheaf.stalk x))
    {Ω : Type u} [Field Ω] [Algebra X.functionField Ω]
    [IsSepClosure X.functionField Ω]
    (G : Type u) [Group G] [TopologicalSpace G] [IsTopologicalGroup G] [CompactSpace G]
    [∀ P, MulAction G ((@fiber X Ω _ (IsSepClosure.sep_closed X.functionField)
      (pointOfAlgebra (K := X.functionField) Ω
        (X.fromSpecStalk (genericPoint X)))).obj P)]
    [IsFundamentalGroup (@fiber X Ω _ (IsSepClosure.sep_closed X.functionField)
      (pointOfAlgebra (K := X.functionField) Ω
        (X.fromSpecStalk (genericPoint X)))) G] :
    (∀ (L : IntermediateField X.functionField Ω) [FiniteDimensional X.functionField L],
      (IsUnramified (X.fromNormalizationIn L) ↔ Etale (X.fromNormalizationIn L)) ∧
      (Etale (X.fromNormalizationIn L) ↔
        IsFinite (X.fromNormalizationIn L) ∧ Etale (X.fromNormalizationIn L))) ∧
    ∃ q : Gal(Ω / X.functionField) →* G,
      q = @galoisToFundamentalGroup X X.functionField Ω _ _ _
        (IsSepClosure.sep_closed X.functionField) (X.fromSpecStalk (genericPoint X))
        _ Ω _ (IsSepClosure.sep_closed X.functionField)
        (pointOfAlgebra (K := X.functionField) Ω (X.fromSpecStalk (genericPoint X)))
        (Iso.refl _) G _ _ _ _ _ _ ∧
      Continuous q ∧ Function.Surjective q ∧
      (∃ M : IntermediateField X.functionField Ω,
        (∀ a : Ω, a ∈ M ↔ ∃ L : IntermediateField X.functionField Ω,
          FiniteDimensional X.functionField L ∧
          IsUnramified (X.fromNormalizationIn L) ∧ a ∈ L) ∧
        IsGalois X.functionField M ∧
        ∃ e : G ≃ₜ* Gal(M / X.functionField),
          ∀ (σ : Gal(Ω / X.functionField)) (a : M), (e (q σ) a : Ω) = σ a) ∧
      (∀ (L : IntermediateField X.functionField Ω)
        [FiniteDimensional X.functionField L] [IsGalois X.functionField L],
        (∃ ρ : G →* Gal(L / X.functionField), Continuous ρ ∧
          ∀ (σ : Gal(Ω / X.functionField)) (a : L), (ρ (q σ) a : Ω) = σ a) ↔
        IsUnramified (X.fromNormalizationIn L)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.FiniteEtale
